#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093330} :activity/id #uuid "3cb60590-ce3c-5ae5-bb4c-9fa261e5f9f2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049934 :topic/id #uuid "1fbdb06d-f1f4-55f3-bf00-af01b659732d" :topic/math-academy-id 992} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093443 :tutorial/content "For a function $f(x),$ its derivative at a point $x$ is defined by the limit\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}.\n$$\n\nBut often, computing this limit exactly is impossible or impractical. One example might be when we don't have an explicit expression for $f(x).$ In such cases, instead of finding an exact value for $f'(x)$, we can *approximate* the derivative using the formula\n\n \n\n$$\nf'(x)\\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}\n$$\n\nfor small values of the \"step size\" $h,$ with $h>0.$\n\nThis expression is called the **forward difference approximation** because if $h >0$ then $x+h$ is a step forward (i.e., to the right) from $x.$\n\n![](images/18/1860d33c3b34fa5c869f5a3c7a04ef4d02271ba30d60f973b892800ef7c9524e.png)\n\nGenerally, this and other similar methods are known as **finite-difference** approximations. The smaller the value of $h,$ the better the approximation." :tutorial/id #uuid "c3fb3f8f-2a89-5f4a-8f24-9cf556a18c41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 363 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c0976a2b-1337-5627-8c4d-ef63079d733d" :step/math-academy-id 676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092386 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091955 :question/id #uuid "a7f1c431-54b4-52b5-9ad6-cb4dd81e0f30" :question/math-academy-id "e-151"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44f09c22-7107-5c80-9c91-a7b58cb639b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #e